e the bar i was happy  on the day  the war began  i was waking up at 5 00 am  i heard  loud explosions  after a while i ran to the atm to  withdraw cash but while  i was standing in line  a bomb fell  two straight away from me  i will never forget that sound  24 year old yaroslav is one of sehi  prakapenko's guara media team  since the war began he and his family  have been sheltering in their second  floor apartment  he's been keeping a video diary  good morning it's  30 minutes past  11  um  16th day of the war  almost all the time we  sit in  between two walls in our house and  almost do nothing  it is  still  heavy  scared and  some kind of angry  i have  a headache  from loud sounds  s30k  and  almost all the day i was spent on the  floor between walls  on my  home  maybe that's all for right now good  night  and  the northern and eastern parts of the  city are under constant bombardment  [Music]  but elsewhere volunteers are helping to  keep people alive  hidden in the back streets of central  harkev this former hostel has been  converted into a community welfare  center  it goes by the name of hell's kitchen  but as the war continued international  aid began to arrive  hell's kitchen now provides food and  medication to over two and a half  thousand people daily  [Music]  and they even have a signature product  with a particular message embedded in  the crust  [Music]  the trident signifies that ukraine is  united and will fight on  [Music]  remember  the food produced here isn't just for  civilians it's hard to cook at the front  line and the hot meals they deliver to  their soldiers are crucial to the war  effort  at an international i.t company and  regularly traveled all over europe  his wife luda also worked in i.t but now  packs delivery boxes from six in the  morning to after midnight  despite the daily attacks the city  authorities have somehow kept essential  utilities operating  [Music]  loading up the van outside hell's  kitchen the volunteers are preparing for  another long and dangerous day  sir he and his camera are going along  for the ride  one of the centre's most active recruits  is andriy gosenko  until a few weeks ago he worked at  heartron a ukrainian aerospace company  he's now leading a rather different life  [Music]  me  by  foreign  foreign  [Music]  the volunteer team head up the five  flights of stairs to the aid of 86 year  old lydia roman ivna  yesterday  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  foreign  morning it's 11 a.m and 20th day of the  war  but yesterday it was  pretty loud day as the day before and  day before  today  my dad and my brother  they went  to the supermarket and buy some food  and now we're still sitting at our house  sometimes we  scream  to each  other  and  [Music]  trying to  [Music]  i hear some explosions  i don't know is it  is it record  and do you hear it  [Music]  also good news a couple days ago from my  colleagues  came some  results  so we shared  when it's free  and trying  to to be safe  a situation is like it was very  intensive shooting in kharkiv center  over the night  i know that several buildings is  destroyed  [Music]  one of those buildings houses one of  ukraine's most important art collections  [Music]  it was damaged when an adjacent police  and security headquarters were targeted  by a russian rocket  but indiscriminate firing is no  respecter of history or culture  well  [Music]  [Music]  art department at harkeev museum of the  arts  it's home to twenty five thousand rare  and valuable exhibits many of which  ironically are russian  specialists  [Music]  [Music]  foreign  [Music]  before the war marina and her husband  lived just a few blocks away from the  museum  but their street and apartment was  heavily bombed and they had to move into  a shelter  she's been trying to come to terms with  what's happened  [Music]  is  [Music]  today  the largest market burning in havakev  this is not  clouds  this is smoke  [Music]  i don't know  if  there is somewhere safe in ukraine now  my friends say they hear  explosions getting close  and  and yeah i'm afraid that the russians  will enter the city because i don't know  what  will happen  and  what to do in this case  but i hope  it  doesn't happen  i wish  for peace  yaroslav and his family don't want to  voluntarily leave their house but for  others one place of at least partial  safety in hakeev is the city's metro  katya parashachanska and her partner  andriy fled here from their home in  northern saltivka  one of the city's worst affected  districts  [Music]  had a population of one and a half  million but over half of the inhabitants  have left  when the fighting began two and a half  thousand people took shelter in this one  metro station  now there are just seven hundred  julia zinko is one of those who stayed  but it's not a healthy environment  but at least every day volunteers bring  supplies to be distributed  blankets clothes and medicines  there's plenty of food and water  and julia has no plans to leave  [Music]  morning  it's  40 minutes after 11  uh 20 of march and  the day number 25  of the  wow  yesterday our electricity was turned off  and  there is no water either  it seems  like  some kind of  joke but  this is our life  and this is  our reality of survival  like the electricity supply the water  supply is erratic  when the water's on they fill every  available container and personal hygiene  becomes a luxury  yesterday wasn't turned on  the water and we used  all the waters that we  have on our base  but today  right now we have a little pressure  uh  i'm sitting on the balcony it's sunny  right now and i hear some  sounds  of spring  i can say  the  like birds have seen him  [Music]  and it's beautiful  [Music]  [Music]  like firefighters medics are in big  demand  andrey guzenko has had an urgent call  from one of his volunteers whose mother  mrs marinich has fallen during a bombing  attack  he's found a doctor and is taking him to  help  [